@@ -235,6 +235,27 @@ The syntax for generating commands is similar to that of [GNU Parallel](https://

 If you do not include a placeholder, *fd* automatically adds a `{}` at the end.

+### Metadata in formatted output
+
+`--format` additionally supports `{%y}` (file, dir, symlink, or other) and `{%s}`
+(filesystem metadata size in bytes). Use the existing `{}` for the path.
+Directory sizes are not recursive totals. With `--follow`, resolved links describe
+their targets; broken links describe the link itself. Unavailable required metadata
+fails output instead of inventing a size. Existing filters may have cached metadata;
+this is not an atomic filesystem snapshot.
+
+For example, in Bash this produces NUL-terminated records with actual tab separators:
+
+```bash
+fd --print0 --format $'{%y}\t{%s}\t{}'
+```
+
+A consumer must split records at NUL, then split only the first two tabs, keeping
+the entire remaining path. This handles spaces, tabs, and newlines in valid UTF-8
+filenames; ordinary line-oriented TSV does not. Existing non-UTF-8 filename
+conversion remains lossy. `--format` does not interpret backslash escapes itself.
+The new placeholders remain literal in `--exec` and `--exec-batch` arguments.
+
 #### Parallel vs. serial execution

 For `-x`/`--exec`, you can control the number of parallel jobs by using the `-j`/`--threads` option.
